# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
#
# The window manager for the recording. i3 rather than a bare X session because
# the scene opens a second window partway through, and something has to decide
# where it goes. A tiling manager decides the same way every take.
#
# No status bar: it would put a clock and a hostname on camera, and neither is
# part of the product. Titlebars and gaps are kept, for the reason below.

font pango:DejaVu Sans Mono 9

# Decorations, deliberately. The first takes had the terminal flush against
# the browser with no border and the same dark palette, and it read as a panel
# inside the tix web interface rather than as a terminal on the same machine.
# A titlebar and a gap cost about fifty pixels and buy the single most
# important thing the frame has to say: these are two different programs.
default_border normal 3
default_floating_border normal 3
title_align center

# The accent is tix's own teal, so the chrome still belongs to the product.
# focused and unfocused are the same on purpose: nothing in the scene takes
# focus deliberately, and a border that changed colour when the mouse moved
# would look like something had happened.
client.focused          #11161d #11161d #d7dce3 #2dd4bf #2dd4bf
client.focused_inactive #11161d #11161d #8b94a3 #39414d #39414d
client.unfocused        #11161d #11161d #8b94a3 #39414d #39414d

gaps inner 10
gaps outer 6

# The browser takes the left, the terminal the right. The board is the thing
# being watched, so it gets the room; the terminal only has to be readable.
workspace_layout default

# Nothing in the scene uses the keyboard through i3, so there are no bindings
# at all. A stray keybinding is a way for a take to go wrong silently.

# Chromium announces itself before its window is mapped; without this it can
# land in the wrong container when the terminal starts first.
assign [class="Chromium"] 1
assign [class="XTerm"] 1

exec --no-startup-id i3-msg 'workspace 1'
